<root>
<problem>
<description><![CDATA[
Count how many 6-digit numbers in a given inclusive range satisfy digit constraints: (1) digits never decrease left-to-right, (2) there exists at least one pair of adjacent equal digits (Part 1), and (3) in Part 2, that adjacent equal pair must belong to a group of exactly two equal digits (not part of a larger group).
]]></description>
<pseudocode>
1. Parse input as a string "L-R"; convert to integers low=L, high=R.
2. Initialize answer = 0.
3. For each number x from low to high (brute force baseline):
   a. Convert x to a 6-digit array digits[0..5] (with leading zeros not needed because range is 6-digit).
   b. Check non-decreasing property:
      - If any digits[i] > digits[i+1], reject x.
   c. Part 1 check (adjacent duplicate exists):
      - If there exists i with digits[i] == digits[i+1], mark hasDouble=true else false.
      - If hasDouble is true, accept x.
   d. Part 2 check (exactly-two group exists):
      - Compute run lengths of equal digits:
        i=0
        while i<6:
          j=i
          while j<6 and digits[j]==digits[i]: j++
          runLen = j-i
          record whether runLen==2
          i=j
      - Accept x only if any runLen==2.
   e. If accepted, answer++.
4. Output answer.
</pseudocode>
<techniques><![CDATA[
- Digit DP / state constraints (conceptual): treat numbers as digit sequences with monotonic constraint and local/group constraints.
- Run-length encoding (RLE) on digits to detect “exactly two” groups efficiently.
- Brute-force with pruning (early rejection): check non-decreasing first to quickly discard most candidates.
]]></techniques>
<planning><![CDATA[
1. Understand constraints:
   - Non-decreasing digits implies the sequence is monotone; this can be checked in O(6).
   - Adjacent equality and “exactly two” are local/group properties; both can be derived from run lengths.
2. Choose approach:
   - For simplicity, brute force over the range is acceptable if the range is not astronomically large; still, implement early pruning.
   - For a more algorithmic approach, use digit generation (construct only non-decreasing sequences) to reduce search space (optional).
3. Implement digit extraction:
   - Convert each candidate number to a 6-digit list.
4. Implement monotonic check:
   - Single pass i=0..4: if digits[i] > digits[i+1], reject immediately.
5. Implement Part 1 detection:
   - Single pass i=0..4: if digits[i]==digits[i+1], accept condition.
6. Implement Part 2 detection:
   - Run-length encode:
     - Walk through digits, count consecutive equal digits.
     - If any run length equals 2, accept.
7. Complexity:
   - Per candidate: O(6) time, O(1) extra space.
   - Total: O((high-low+1)*6) time for brute force.
   - With pruning, many candidates are rejected quickly.
]]></planning>
</problem>

<problem>
<description><![CDATA[
Given a range [L, R], count numbers whose digits are non-decreasing and contain at least one adjacent equal pair (similar to Part 1), ignoring the “exactly two” restriction.
]]></description>
<pseudocode>
1. Read input "L-R"; set low, high.
2. answer = 0
3. For x in [low..high]:
   a. digits = toDigits6(x)
   b. if not nonDecreasing(digits): continue
   c. if hasAdjacentEqual(digits): answer++
4. Print answer
5. Helper nonDecreasing(digits):
   - for i in 0..4:
       if digits[i] > digits[i+1]: return false
   - return true
6. Helper hasAdjacentEqual(digits):
   - for i in 0..4:
       if digits[i] == digits[i+1]: return true
   - return false
</pseudocode>
<techniques><![CDATA[
- Early pruning with monotonic check.
- Linear scan for adjacent equality.
- Constant-size digit processing (fixed length 6).
]]></techniques>
<planning><![CDATA[
1. Reduce the problem to two independent checks:
   - Monotonicity (global constraint).
   - Existence of adjacent duplicate (local constraint).
2. Implement monotonicity first:
   - It’s cheap and rejects quickly.
3. Implement adjacent duplicate check:
   - Another cheap linear scan.
4. Complexity:
   - O(6) per number, O(1) space.
   - Total O(rangeSize) time.
5. Use cases:
   - Counting constrained digit patterns in numeric ranges.
   - Baseline approach before optimizing with digit generation or digit DP.
]]></planning>
</problem>

