]]></algorithm>

<algorithm_tutorial>
A generic tutorial for this type of “digit constraint counting in a range” problem:

1. Key observation (monotonic constraint):
   - If digits never decrease, then the sequence is monotone non-decreasing.
   - That means you can generate valid digit sequences directly instead of checking every number in the range.

2. State representation:
   - Build the number left-to-right.
   - Maintain:
     - position idx (0..5),
     - last digit chosen (to enforce non-decreasing),
     - run-length information (for Part 2) or a boolean “has adjacent equal” (for Part 1).

3. Backtracking / DFS generation:
   - At each position idx, choose the next digit d such that d >= lastDigit.
   - This prunes huge portions of the search space because decreasing sequences are never generated.

4. Handling Part 1 vs Part 2:
   - Part 1: track whether any adjacent equal occurs.
     - When you choose d == lastDigit, you’ve formed an adjacent pair.
   - Part 2: track run lengths of equal digits.
     - When the digit changes, finalize the previous run length.
     - Accept only if at least one run length equals exactly 2.

5. Range filtering:
   - Because you generate digit sequences, you can:
     - Either generate all monotone sequences of length 6 and then count those whose numeric value lies in [L, R],
     - Or incorporate tight bounds (more complex) by tracking whether the prefix is already above/below the bounds.
   - For simplicity, generate all monotone sequences (there are not many for length 6) and then filter by numeric range.

6. Efficiency:
   - Brute force: O(rangeSize * 6).
   - Digit generation:
     - Number of non-decreasing sequences of length 6 over digits 0..9 is C(6+10-1, 6)=C(15,6)=5005 (small).
     - Each sequence is processed in O(6).
     - Total work is ~O(5005 * 6), effectively constant for this puzzle size.

7. Use cases:
   - Counting numbers with monotonic digit constraints.
   - Problems where adjacency/group properties can be tracked incrementally during construction.
   - Any “fixed-length digit DP” variant where constraints are local or can be summarized by small state.

</algorithm_tutorial>

<learned_techniques><![CDATA[
- Run-length encoding (RLE) on digits to detect “exactly two adjacent digits” groups efficiently.
- Early pruning by checking the monotonic (non-decreasing) constraint before more expensive checks.
- Digit sequence generation/backtracking under monotonic constraints to drastically reduce the search space compared to brute force.
]]></learned_techniques>
</root>
